---
/**
 * VERBATIM legal text from the Notion page "Privacy Policy & Terms of Service" (Privacy Policy last changed November 11, 2026).
 * Do not edit copy here. Edit the Notion page, then mirror the change.
 */
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import LegalBanner from '../components/LegalBanner.astro';

const title = 'Sondor privacy policy, and where your words actually live';
const description = 'What Sondor collects, what it does not, and where your content is stored. Your transcripts and pieces stay in your own Notion. Sondor holds account records.';
---
<Layout {title} {description}>
  <section class="section page-head hero-field">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Legal</p>
      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <LegalBanner />
      <p class="muted">Last updated: November 11, 2026 (Privacy Policy: deleting your account, and more detail on feedback, error reports and imported files)</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section--tight">
    <div class="container narrow prose legal">

      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <p>Sondor helps you write using your own words. The material you create, your interview transcripts, your stories, your finished pieces, is stored in <strong>your own Notion workspace</strong>, under your own Notion account. Sondor's servers also keep some of your words, because a few features need them. Exactly what, and for how long, is below.</p>
      <p>We do hold some things: your account details, the secure connection to your Notion, and records needed to run the service. Details below.</p>

      <h2>What we collect</h2>
      <p><strong>Content you create</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Interview transcripts and session material</li>
        <li>Your voice when you dictate, turned into text (Sondor doesn't keep the audio)</li>
        <li>Audio and video files and YouTube links you import for transcription</li>
        <li>Writing samples and documents you upload or paste</li>
        <li>Content generated from your own words</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Account and operational data</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Your Notion account and workspace IDs (you sign in with Notion; Sondor has no password of its own)</li>
        <li>The email address on your Notion account, which Sondor reads from Notion when needed rather than storing it</li>
        <li>If you applied to the beta: the name, email and answers from your application</li>
        <li>Your encrypted Notion connection token</li>
        <li>Sign-in records (when you signed in and were last active)</li>
        <li>A random install ID the app creates on your device, so sessions on two devices don't clash (not an advertising or hardware ID)</li>
        <li>Subscription and payment status</li>
        <li>Usage records (sessions completed, features used)</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>What we don't collect</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>We don't track you across other websites</li>
        <li>We don't sell your data to anyone</li>
        <li>We don't use your content to train AI models</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where your data actually lives</h2>
      <p>This distinction matters, so we're being precise about it:</p>
      <p><strong>In your own Notion workspace:</strong> your content. Interview transcripts, stories, voice profile, finished pieces, everything Sondor helps you produce. You own it, you can read it directly in Notion without us, and you keep it if you leave.</p>
      <p><strong>On Sondor's servers:</strong> your account record, your encrypted Notion access token, sign-in records, subscription status, and usage data. This is what's needed to log you in, connect you to your own Notion, and bill you correctly. Usage records also show us how Sondor is used, so we can improve it.</p>
      <p>Some of your words are kept on Sondor's servers too:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>While a session is in progress, a copy of it, so you can pause and pick it up on any device. It's deleted when the session ends, or when the piece is finished, archived or deleted.</li>
        <li>Your answers to a piece's questions, and the passages Sondor's voice learning last read from your writing, so it doesn't learn the same words twice. These stay until you delete your account.</li>
        <li>Any verification page you choose to publish. It's public by design and stays up until you delete your account.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Feedback you send us goes to Sondor's own Notion workspace with details that identify you, so we can reply: your display name, your Sondor account ID and your Notion workspace's name, plus the name and email from your beta application if you sent one.</p>

      <h2>Third parties we use</h2>
      <p>Your data passes through these services in the course of running Sondor:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Anthropic (Claude)</strong>: processes your session material to conduct interviews and assemble content. Under Anthropic's API terms, material submitted through their API is not used to train their models.</li>
        <li><strong>Notion</strong>: stores your content, in your own workspace, under your own Notion account and their privacy policy.</li>
        <li><strong>AssemblyAI</strong>: transcribes voice into text. Dictation goes from your device straight to AssemblyAI; imported audio and video are sent by our servers.</li>
        <li><strong>Firecrawl</strong>: when you import a YouTube video, receives its link so we can get the audio.</li>
        <li><strong>YouTube</strong>: when you import a video, our servers fetch its captions and details.</li>
        <li><strong>Stripe</strong>: processes payments. We never see or store your full card details.</li>
        <li><strong>Railway</strong>: hosts our backend servers.</li>
        <li><strong>Cloudflare</strong>: serves our website and the web app.</li>
        <li><strong>Google Play</strong>: on Android, shows the prompt asking you to rate Sondor.</li>
        <li><strong>Sentry</strong>: receives error reports from our servers, tagged with your account ID. We remove request contents before a report is sent.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Voice recordings</h2>
      <p>When you record audio in Sondor, it's transcribed to text so the app can work with your words. Sondor doesn't keep recordings. When you dictate, your audio streams from your device to AssemblyAI and only the text comes back. Audio and video files you import are sent by our servers to AssemblyAI to be transcribed. Sondor doesn't store the files or links you import; the transcript goes to your Notion. AssemblyAI and Firecrawl handle them under their own policies.</p>

      <h2>Sensitive material</h2>
      <p>Sondor's interviews can go into personal territory, formative experiences, loss, belief, unresolved tension. That's the point of the tool.</p>
      <p>Because of that, Sondor includes a safeguard: if a session surfaces language suggesting serious distress, the app will show you crisis resource information, and will mark that material so it isn't automatically resurfaced to you later in suggestions or prompts. The material stays fully visible in your own archive, nothing is hidden or deleted from you. It simply isn't served back unprompted.</p>
      <p>This detection is automated and imperfect. It is not monitoring by a human, and it is not a substitute for real support.</p>

      <h2>Your Notion connection</h2>
      <p>You connect your own Notion account to Sondor by granting access, and you choose what to share. You can revoke that access at any time from Notion's own settings.</p>
      <p>If you revoke it, Sondor loses access, but <strong>your content stays exactly where it is</strong>, in your Notion, untouched. Nothing gets deleted, because it was never ours to delete.</p>

      <h2>Deleting your account</h2>
      <p>You can delete your account at any time: in the app under Settings > Account, or on the web at <a href="https://sondor.app/delete-account">sondor.app/delete-account</a>. If you need help, email <a href="mailto:support@sondor.ai">support@sondor.ai</a>.</p>
      <p>Deleting your account removes it right away, along with what Sondor's servers keep with it: your account record, your stored Notion connection token, sign-in, usage and billing records, sessions in progress, your answers to pieces' questions, the passages voice learning has read, any verification pages you've published, and your beta application if you sent one. Your payment details are removed from Stripe, which keeps a record of past payments.</p>
      <p>A few things stay:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Feedback you've sent, with the details that identify you removed.</li>
        <li>A one-way code made from your Notion workspace ID, kept only so a new account on the same workspace doesn't get a second free trial.</li>
        <li>Error reports and server logs, which are deleted on their usual schedule, within 90 days.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If you have a paid plan, deleting your account ends it immediately and the rest of the period isn't refunded. To use the time you've paid for, cancel your plan first and delete your account after it ends.</p>
      <p>Your content in Notion is unaffected, it's yours, and it stays.</p>

      <h2>If Sondor shuts down</h2>
      <p>Sondor is an early-stage product built by one person. If it ever ceases operation, your content is unaffected: it lives in your Notion workspace and remains fully accessible to you without Sondor existing. We'd give reasonable advance notice and delete account data from our servers.</p>

      <h2>Age requirement</h2>
      <p>Sondor is for adults, 18 and over. The interviews explore personal history, belief, and formative experience at real depth, and the product isn't designed or appropriate for minors. We don't knowingly collect data from anyone under 18.</p>

      <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
      <p>If we change this policy meaningfully, we'll notify you by email before the changes take effect.</p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>Questions about privacy: <a href="mailto:hello@sondor.ai">hello@sondor.ai</a></p>

      <p class="muted cross">See also the <a href="/terms">Terms of Service</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-head { padding-block: var(--s-20) var(--s-10); }
  .page-head h1 { margin-block-start: var(--s-3); }
  .legal { padding-block-end: var(--s-16); }
  .cross { margin-block-start: var(--s-12); }
</style>
